Network Config Manager
======================

This repository is a fork of:

    https://github.com/justinthoms/network-config-manager

forked at commit 72b5cf5, and originally written by Justin Thoms. The work is
his; this fork exists to make the project integrable with Company OS, and the
upstream project remains the source of everything that was already here.


On the licence file
-------------------

Upstream's README states "MIT License" but the repository carries no LICENSE
file. The MIT text was added here to record the licence the author already
declared — not to assert any terms of our own over his work. The copyright line
names Justin Thoms for that reason.

If upstream adds its own LICENSE, that file is the authority and this fork
should adopt it verbatim.


Modifications in this fork
--------------------------

1. `GET /health` and `GET /ready` (`backend/app/api/health.py`).
   Unauthenticated. `/health` deliberately checks nothing so it stays truthful
   when a dependency is down; `/ready` checks the database. Offered upstream as
   a courtesy pull request — this fork does not depend on it being accepted.

2. Identity-provider tokens accepted alongside the local login
   (`backend/app/core/oidc.py`, `backend/app/api/deps.py`).
   Off unless `OIDC_ISSUER` and `OIDC_AUDIENCE` are both set. The local
   username-and-password login is unchanged and remains the default path. A user
   arriving on a valid token is provisioned as `viewer`; roles are granted by an
   administrator here, never asserted by a claim in someone else's token.

3. Pagination and filtering on `GET /api/devices/`, plus `GET /api/devices/{id}`
   (`backend/app/api/devices.py`).
   Every parameter is optional and the default is the previous behaviour, so
   existing callers — including this project's own UI — are unaffected.

4. Browser sign-in through an identity provider (`backend/app/api/sso.py`, and
   a button plus a fragment handler in `backend/app/web/index.html`).
   Off unless configured. Authorization-code flow with `state`, PKCE and a
   `nonce`; the callback mints *this application's own* access token, so every
   route, permission check and the existing page carry on unchanged.

   Where it is configured it is the way in: an unauthenticated visitor is sent
   straight to the provider rather than shown a password box for an account they
   do not have. The local login is not removed -- `/?local=1` always reaches it,
   which is what gets you in when the provider is down. A failed sign-in returns
   to the page with a reason and does not redirect again, so a broken provider
   cannot become a loop.

5. A missing dashboard element (`backend/app/web/index.html`).
   `renderDashboard()` writes into `#dashboardBackups`, which the page did not
   contain, so it threw partway through and everything after it -- recent
   backups, cloud integrations, the roadmap -- never rendered. The panel is
   restored. Worth offering upstream.

6. Packaging: a Dockerfile and a compose file (see below).

7. This NOTICE and the LICENSE described above.


Deliberately not changed
------------------------

The role model, the absence of Alembic, and the backup engine. None is needed for
integration, and each would make this fork harder to keep current with upstream.

The frontend was not replaced either: browser sign-in added a button, a link and
about twenty lines to the existing page.
